From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 23:20:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] DIAGNOSTIC: remoteproc: qcom: read the crash reason on demand

Not for source= beyond the investigation it belongs to.

qcom_q6v5 reads the remote's crash reason out of SMEM in exactly one place,
the fatal interrupt handler. On this device an LTE attach kills the SoC
about ten seconds after the modem finishes programming its GSI channels and
no fatal ever arrives -- the interrupt count stays at zero -- so the item is
never read.

Reading it after the fact does not work either. Patch 0073 did that at probe
and found the item empty every time, including after a reset the bootloader
itself labelled a watchdog, because the bootloader reinitialises SMEM on
every boot.

The one window where the item could hold something is while the machine is
still running, between the modem deciding it is unhappy and the chip going
down. Export it so it can be sampled through the attach, four times a second,
into a log that survives the reset.

If it stays empty right up to the last sample, the modem never got as far as
writing a reason, and the thing that ends this is not a modem assert.
---
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
@@ -245,6 +245,62 @@
  *
  * Return: 0 on success, negative errno on failure
  */
+/* DIAGNOSTIC: read the remote's crash reason on demand.
+ *
+ * The driver only looks at this SMEM item when the fatal interrupt arrives.
+ * On this device an LTE attach kills the SoC about ten seconds after the modem
+ * finishes programming its channels and no fatal ever arrives, so the item is
+ * never read, and reading it after the fact is useless because the bootloader
+ * reinitialises SMEM on every boot.
+ *
+ * The one window where it could hold something is while the machine is still
+ * running. Export it so it can be sampled during the attach.
+ */
+struct q6v5_crash_attr {
+	struct device_attribute attr;
+	struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5;
+};
+
+static ssize_t crash_reason_text_show(struct device *dev,
+				      struct device_attribute *attr, char *buf)
+{
+	struct q6v5_crash_attr *ca;
+	size_t len;
+	char *msg;
+
+	ca = container_of(attr, struct q6v5_crash_attr, attr);
+
+	if (!ca->q6v5->crash_reason)
+		return sysfs_emit(buf, "(no smem id)\n");
+
+	msg = qcom_smem_get(QCOM_SMEM_HOST_ANY, ca->q6v5->crash_reason, &len);
+	if (IS_ERR(msg))
+		return sysfs_emit(buf, "(err %ld)\n", PTR_ERR(msg));
+	if (!len || !msg[0])
+		return sysfs_emit(buf, "-\n");
+
+	return sysfs_emit(buf, "%.*s\n", (int)len, msg);
+}
+
+static void q6v5_crash_attr_init(struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5,
+				 struct platform_device *pdev)
+{
+	struct q6v5_crash_attr *ca;
+
+	ca = devm_kzalloc(&pdev->dev, sizeof(*ca), GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!ca)
+		return;
+
+	sysfs_attr_init(&ca->attr.attr);
+	ca->attr.attr.name = "crash_reason_text";
+	ca->attr.attr.mode = 0444;
+	ca->attr.show = crash_reason_text_show;
+	ca->q6v5 = q6v5;
+
+	if (device_create_file(&pdev->dev, &ca->attr))
+		dev_warn(&pdev->dev, "no crash_reason_text\n");
+}
+
 int qcom_q6v5_init(struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5, struct platform_device *pdev,
 		   struct rproc *rproc, int crash_reason, const char *load_state,
 		   void (*handover)(struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5))
@@ -256,6 +312,8 @@
 	q6v5->crash_reason = crash_reason;
 	q6v5->handover = handover;
 
+	q6v5_crash_attr_init(q6v5, pdev);
+
 	init_completion(&q6v5->start_done);
 	init_completion(&q6v5->stop_done);
 
